# core v3.9 — WARNING. An empty family name.
#
# THE EMPTY STRING IS WHAT A FORM WITH AN UNTOUCHED FIELD WRITES. A serializer
# that reads a text input and emits whatever it holds emits "" for a field the
# user never filled in, and the triple that reaches the pod is a claim the user
# never made: it says the person's family name IS the empty string, which is a
# different fact from having no family name recorded. Nothing downstream can
# tell the two apart afterwards, because both produce a blank in every rendering
# — and only one of them means "unknown".
#
# sh:minLength 1 is what notices it. That is the whole of what this constraint
# buys, and it is why the three name properties carry a minimum length at all
# when none of them is required: absence is fine and says nothing, presence with
# no content is a defect and says something false.
#
# THIS FIXTURE IS `.WARN.` AND NOT `.INVALID.`, AND THAT IS THE CORE v3.5
# RATCHET, NOT A HEDGE. A constraint on a fact no producer has yet had the
# opportunity to write enters at sh:Warning, so a pod carrying an empty name is
# REPORTED and never rejected — a profile is not thrown away over a blank field,
# and the rest of it (date of birth, contact, address) stays readable. Filing
# this as `.INVALID.ttl` would fail with NO_VIOLATION and would assert a
# severity core v3.9 explicitly declined to take.
#
# foaf:givenName and foaf:name are both present and well formed here, so the
# node is not otherwise deficient and exactly one thing is under test.
#
# cascade:dateOfBirth is what reaches it. See extended-profile-name.VALID.ttl.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<#me>
    foaf:givenName "Alex" ;
    foaf:familyName "" ;
    foaf:name "Alex Rivera" ;
    cascade:dateOfBirth "1990-01-01"^^xsd:date .
